So the new main concepts that I want to teach in this video

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:00.760
is the H runtime environment.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.640
So what's H runtime?

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.360
The H runtime is if you come back to exactly the Versailles docs

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:07.520
that we saw just now.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.000
So the H runtime is in comparison to the normal Node.js environment.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.440
So H runtime is, let me draw it by this explanation.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.120
So imagine this whole thing is the globe, is the whole world, right?

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.920
And let's say I have these two continents, Asia and America.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.200
Obviously, they're not to scale and there are other continents,

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.880
but I'll demonstrate something.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.640
So let's say when you deploy a normal function,

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.720
a normal endpoint to Versailles or a normal server,

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.120
the server will leave somewhere in the world that you choose

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.800
that is closest to your users.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.280
So let's say a lot of your users are close to Asia.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.560
You ideally set up your server in Asia

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.480
so that whoever makes requests to the server

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.440
will be able to quickly get the response back

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.920
so to ensure the best user experience.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.440
However, what if the someone from America

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.200
wants to make a request to your server?

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.640
It's to travel all the way down to Asia and then get the response back.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.440
So you make the whole experience very slow.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.720
So that's the traditional serverless environment thing.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:07.080
So what the H runtime does is instead of packaging up

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.880
and deploying the server to only one location,

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:12.560
it actually is able to duplicate the code

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:17.200
and actually deploy the execution environment onto CDNs.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:19.520
So CDNs are content delivery networks,

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.600
meaning they are small machines

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.440
that is distributed all around the world, right?

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.680
And basically it allows for a very fast user experience.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.120
So whenever, wherever your users are,

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.640
they are able to find a CDN that's closest to them.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:34.960
And so when they make the request,

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:37.440
no matter where you are in the world, it's going to be super fast.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:40.920
So why the trade-off of being able to deploy all around the world

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:42.240
and making it super fast,

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.960
the trade-off is that H runtime usually have a smaller package size.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.000
Meaning, let's say we come back to the H runtime.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.600
Let's look at that trade-off.

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:53.120
So H functions run on H runtime, right?

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.120
So the H runtime does not expose all the node APIs.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:00.520
Meaning, right, the power of node.js is that it has a lot of packages on NPM, right?

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:05.320
So you're able to use all the NPM packages on a normal serverless environment, right?

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:07.160
But if you're using the H runtime,

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:09.400
you're sacrificing the speed for,

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.880
you're sacrificing the packages for the speed.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:16.080
So there will be a lot of restriction in terms of the APIs that you're able to use.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.080
But if you sacrifice that and you use the supported APIs

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:22.040
like the fetch API and the request response, all these,

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:23.320
if you're able to sacrifice that,

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:28.640
you're able to get a very, very fast response time for your application, right?

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.600
So the reason why I brought up the H runtime is because

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:37.080
today I'll be teaching you how to deploy all your AI services on the H runtime.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:40.600
So OpenAI has actually created a lot of initiatives

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:42.720
and a lot of these are different packages,

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:44.480
especially the Versailles AI SDK.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:49.040
They have started a lot of initiatives to move these AI packages up to the H,

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:50.520
to make it H compatible.

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:54.840
Meaning that a lot of these AI applications will be able to run on the H, right?

